  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044176/assets/25717d521feeec25f7b4b722ca7b8a4ee9b5fdc8423bac06953e5f2adc8eef3d.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $t=\\lambda r+\\mu s$, find $\\lambda $ and $\\mu .$",
  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044176/assets/d585c949d7de8cc3fa2d62bfb60990af12242c32ef3390e563f7e627844c5a88.png)\n\nIn $\u25b3ORT$ shown above, we have:\n\n- $OR=3|r|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OR}$ and $r$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OR}=3r.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $RT=2|s|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{RT}$ has the opposite direction to $s$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{RT}=-2s.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =\\overset{\\to }{OR}+\\overset{\\to }{RT} \\\\  & =3r-2s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $t=\\lambda r+\\mu s=3r-2s.$ \n\nSince $s\u2226r,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =3$ and $\\mu =-2$",
